<!--
    Cast campaign, lever `elemptr`: a pointer the program uses only as an array
    of one element type is declared as a pointer to that element.

    A table lookup `t[i]` lowers to INT_ADD(t, i) feeding a LOAD, and
    TypeOpIntAdd votes an integer for both operands and will not carry the
    element's pointer back over the add (typeop.cc:1217), so a textbook base64
    decoder reads, with the option off,

        void * w_decode(int8 a0,uint8 a1,uint8 *a2)
        ((char *)dat_300053e0)[*(uint1 *)(v2 + 0x30005080L)] = (char)v2;

    its input `long`, its output buffer `void *`, the table it allocated and
    keeps in a global an integer behind a cast, and the constant alphabet in
    .data an address behind another.

    Stage / sub-stage: P5 "type-propagation". The P0 assertion
    `option elemptr on|off` decides whether a pointer-width value whose every
    access is one element width through an index the program computes gets a
    `T *` vote in the getLocalType fold.

    The fixture is decompiler/crates/kuna-analysis/tests/fixtures/elemptr_x86_64.c
    built by gcc -O0 non-PIE at 0x30000000 with its data symbols dropped:

        w_decode: the witness - input, output buffer and global table indexed
        w_build:  fills the global table through an index read from the .data
                  alphabet, a constant indexed by a computed value
        w_sidx:   a byte array whose signed elements index an int array
        w_table:  a .data table of ints read with a computed index
        w_record: a control - `p[2 * i] * p[2 * i + 1]` walks 16-byte records,
                  so no element width is claimed
        w_mixed:  a control - `p[i] + *(int *)(p + 4)` reads one pointer at
                  two widths, a record and not an array
        w_wu:     returns an element of an `unsigned short` .data table that
                  nothing else reads: the element is unsigned (its callers
                  widen it), never a signed default
        w_put:    a control - copies into a buffer bounded by a length it
                  also compares a pointer difference against: `buf + len`
                  has the buffer as its base, so the length stays a number
        w_ctr:    stores an `int` counter into an `unsigned` table: the
                  counter keeps its own sign, so its compares need no cast
        w_hdr:    reads a 2-byte field at +4 through a pointer its callee
                  `w_sum4` (declared here `int8 w_sum4(uint4 *p, int8 n)`)
                  reads as `uint4 *`.  Upstream's RuleExpandLoad widens the
                  load to the 4-byte element and truncates it,
                  `(uint2)a0[1]`, reading two bytes the program never reads;
                  with the option on a pointee inferred from use keeps the
                  load 2 bytes wide
        w_gpbump: steps a global `int *` by 4 bytes (`gp++`), and
        w_tfirst: reads the first element of a `.data` table by name: both
                  print that integer form in both passes.  A batch
                  (`decompile-all`, `decompile-project`) never lets another
                  function type the global or name the table an array, since
                  the one declaration would rescale this byte step or make
                  this scalar the array; one function at a time has no batch,
                  and the compiled round trip in decompile_all_cli.rs checks
                  the batch.  Several functions decompiled with no batch to
                  agree -- a --jobs worker, a streamed export, a run that does
                  not take the callee-first order -- type no global or table
        w_nexttab: coreutils expand's get_next_tab_column - loads an element
                  of a global table through an address computed in the rax it
                  returns that element in; typed, the address and the
                  returned element merged into one variable and the function
                  was declared to return `uint8 *`.  It returns `uint8` in
                  both passes
        w_pick:   merges its parameter with a 15-byte .rodata table that
                  holds a zero byte, then hashes `n` bytes through the merged
                  pointer: with the option on the parameter is `char *` and
                  the table stays its address, `&dat_30003020`, never the
                  string literal "0!0\t\x06..." that would end at that zero
                  byte while the reader takes all fifteen.  A caller passing
                  the table to a callee whose parameter the option typed is
                  the same shape across two functions, which only a batch
                  sees; tests/cli and the compiled round trip in
                  decompile_all_cli.rs cover it

    Pass 1 (`option elemptr off`) reproduces the casts and pass 2 (`on`) is the
    fix. The two controls are identical in both passes.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">
c3
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="harness_stub"/>
</binaryimage>
